            <ArticleTitle>Synthesis and preliminary in vitro biological evaluation of 7a-testosterone-chlorambucil hybrid designed for the treatment of prostate cancer.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>442-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.ejmech.2013.04.027</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0223-5234(13)00259-6</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The synthesis of 7a-testosterone-chlorambucil hybrid is reported. This compound is made from testosterone in a 6 step reaction sequence and with 23% overall yield. An alternative convergent reaction sequence yielded the same hybrid through a Grubbs metathesis reaction between chlorambucil allyl ester and 7a-allyltestosterone with 35% overall yield. MTT assays showed that the hybrid is selective towards hormone-dependent prostate cancer cell line (LNCaP (AR+)) and shows similar activity than the parent drug, chlorambucil. Thus, the new hybrid shows promising potential for drug targeting of hormone-dependent prostate cancer through its capacity of delivering chlorambucil directly to the site of treatment. This could extend the use of chlorambucil to prostate cancer in the future.</AbstractText>
